=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk ticket's premise is incorrect — router4 is fully operational and there is no fault to remediate.

Direct inspection of router4 shows that its LAN-facing interface eth0 is UP and correctly bound to 192.168.200.1/24 (the Location C default gateway). Its uplink interfaces eth4 (192.168.15.2/24, toward router5) and eth5 (172.25.1.1/24, toward router3) are also UP. The FRR RIP daemon is running and has exchanged a complete routing table: router4 holds RIP-learned routes to all remote subnets (192.168.1.0/24, 192.168.100.0/24, 172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24, 10.x.x.x blocks) with valid next-hops and reasonable metrics. The kernel FIB mirrors the RIP table with no rejected or queued entries.

From the PC side, both pc11 (192.168.200.11) and pc12 (192.168.200.12) — representative Location C end-hosts — ping their default gateway 192.168.200.1 with 0 % packet loss and sub-2 ms RTTs. They also successfully reach remote sites (192.168.1.1 and 192.168.100.1) through router4, confirming end-to-end forwarding is intact.

There is no anomaly anywhere in the Location C path. Router4 was never down; it is healthy, its gateway is reachable, and cross-site routing works normally. The ticket is a false premise — the "outage" reported by Location C users does not correspond to any actual network failure in the live lab.

No corrective action is needed.
=== END ===